[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read by tenant and body, its reports are two problems.
The queued parser fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own cause and needs its own ticket and fix.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Counted the desk's way, with the desk's own view as exported on 4 October: one report per id (the
export lists some reports twice), placed by tenant and channel, not by subject or intake tag.

## One ticket per problem

<TicketSplit ticket={4471} tickets={{ flip: 1, shift: 3 }} />

Tickets count reports as the ticket system exported them for #4471. The export comes in pages that
overlap, so some reports are listed twice; each row is counted, and the report ids column counts each
report once.

### Day/month flip: dates read month first

Ticket: **#1**. The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants all write the day first,
so a booking for 7 April comes out as 4 July. It reaches every tenant, through the app, by email and
through both booking forms. This is the bug #4471 was opened for, and the fix that is queued.

The booking-v3 form reports, the desk's `hint` row, show the same flip. They were filed as #2 and
closed as a duplicate of #1. The desk counts them in their own row, so #1's count leaves them out.

### One-day move: the booking lands a day off

Ticket: **#3**. These are the reports the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) but filed
under `parser` by tenant. They are not a flip. The date moves by one day ("Tuesday 6 October showed
as Monday"), the same meeting falls on different days in Lisbon and Toronto, and an invite arrives
before its booking. A day/month swap cannot do that: it keeps the date or moves it to another month.

**Not confirmed.** A time-zone or daylight-saving conversion of the invite would fit. Nobody has
checked the code yet. Look at how invite dates are converted between the tenant's and the recipient's
time zone before calling it that.

## How to read a report

- Place a report by its tenant's locale and by what its body says, not by its subject.
  The same subjects come in for both problems above.
- Do not use the intake tag. It follows the reporter's browser, not the cause.

